## What We Did

The session ran the full BI[6] feature pipeline from grill to ship:
- Phase 0 (grill): 5 questions locked in plain English (Q1 ownership, Q2 scope, Q3 permission naming, Q4 package location, Q5 registration wiring).
- Phase 0.5 (uncertainty scan): U1-U7 classified; U2 (`system.health` depth) defaulted to always-ok in v1.
- Phase 1 (PRD): 142 lines, 7 acceptance criteria.
- Phase 2 (TRD): 386 lines, including the authz wildcard fix and the 4-call migration strategy.
- Phase 3 (FLOW): 292 lines, 6 flows + QA checklist.
- Phase 4 (IMPL): 326 lines, 6 phases.
- Phase 5a (stability check): cross-doc consistency review. Caught one counting discrepancy (TRD §4.2 stutter) and one perf N+1 smell (CLI filters) — both fixed.
- Phase 5b (implement): 6 phases, 308/308 tests pass, 0 lint errors, build clean.
- Phase 5c (gap-report review): feature-pipeline-review sub-agent found 0 RED, 5 YELLOW. All 4 follow-ups resolved: AC-3 happy-path test added, drift 1/2 docs updated, session.list caveat added.
- Phase 6 (ship): 3 commits, 2 via PR #6 (merged).

## What Was Shipped

- **`@platform/platform-capabilities`** (new package, 5 files, 14 tests)
- **`gateway-core` augmentations**: 3-line authz wildcard fix, 9 new handlers (plugin.* + system.*), SESSION_LESS_CAPABILITIES set extended
- **`@platform/agentide` CLI**: `--owner` and `--tier` filters on `capability list`
- **Doc pack**: 5 docs (GRILL, PRD, TRD, FLOW, IMPL) totalling 1141 lines
- **Backlog**: BI[6] row shipped (status, what, impl, notes fields updated)
- **HTML graph**: Tier 2 progress line updated, gateway-core notes field updated to reflect post-BI[6] state

## Key Decisions Made

- **Authz wildcard**: 3-line fix in `tierCovers` makes `platform.*.read` cover every read-tier platform cap. Discovered this was broken during TRD review.
- **4-call migration**: re-register `gateway` with only its 12 caps (registry diff removes 7 legacy caps); then 3 calls for session-manager, capability-registry, plugin-manager. The only path that handles the cross-owner collision on upgrade.
- **`platform-capabilities` is a registration seam only**: handlers live in gateway-core. The new package owns the *shape* of every platform cap (name, version, permissions, description, owner) but not the runtime implementations.

## Known Caveats

- **Downgrade is not supported**: rollback to pre-BI[6] would fail the cross-owner collision check. Documented in TRD §4.2. A downgrade requires a manual `agentide capability reset` step (out of scope for v1).
- **`session.list` returns `[]` placeholder**: SessionManager v1 doesn't expose `listSessions(tenantId)`. v2 enhancement.
- **CLI filters are O(N+1)**: iterate `list()` → `describe()` per cap. v1 has ~30 caps; sub-millisecond. Future `registry.listByOwner()` helper if catalog grows.

## Next Pack

The next pack is **BI[7] permission-tiering**. Its docs are not yet drafted. The CLI already filters by tier (read/write), but the actual scope convention enforcement platform-wide is BI[7]'s scope. Per the BACKLOG IMPL:

> Implement the `read`/`act`/`destructive` scope convention platform-wide (not just documented).

Run `feature-pipeline` from Phase 0 (grill). The authz.ts wildcard fix from BI[6] is a useful precedent for BI[7] — the authz already supports tier-hierarchy (`read < act < destructive` for runtime, `read < write` for platform), but BI[7] probably needs to extend the namespace-scoping further (e.g., `runtime.*.act` covering `runtime.*.read` cross-namespace, similar to `platform.*.read` in BI[6]).

To pick up BI[7]:
1. `cd agentide && npm install` (use `npm`, not `pnpm` — documented in BI[6] handoff)
2. `npm run test` — confirm 308/308 baseline
3. Create `docs/features/permission-tiering/`
4. Start the grill with the BI[7] PRD surface

Skill load order for next session: `caveman`, `log-prompt`, `situation-awareness` (reads this handoff directly), `feature-pipeline` (Phase 0).

